Malnutrition
Geriatric & Frailty
Unintentional rapid weight loss, Visible fat/muscle wasting, Poor oral intake, Positive MNA screening

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-029
Reason for Visit: Extreme lethargy, acute inability to mobilize, and newly developed severe swelling in the lower legs in a profoundly cachectic patient.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and concerned neighbor
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a frail patient who had stopped eating for several weeks, recently resulting in extreme weakness and new-onset, severe bilateral leg swelling. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to evaluate for severe hypoproteinaemia (albumin deficiency) causing third-spacing, rule out acute heart failure, and prevent irreversible metabolic collapse prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I have no energy to even lift a spoon, and my legs have blown up like balloons over the last three days."
History of Present Illness: The neighbor reports the patient has suffered unintentional profound weight loss, visually estimated to be well exceeding 10% over the last 6 months. The patient admits to poor oral intake and a severe loss of appetite over several weeks, stating food "tastes like cardboard." They report generalized profound fatigue and a significant loss of physical energy, leading to an acute inability to mobilize today. The neighbor noted the sudden development of severe peripheral oedema in the lower legs. The patient describes severe dental pain from ill-fitting dentures, which has severely impeded mastication and contributed heavily to the starvation. They report recent delayed wound healing of minor skin tears. They deny acute chest pain, orthopnoea, or paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound due to profound weakness and heavy, swollen legs; usually requires a stick.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of imminent physiological collapse, severe pressure injuries, and social isolation contributing to neglect.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 | HR: 65 | Temp: 36.2 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 46 Kg
General: Profoundly cachectic, lethargic, pale, with sunken cheeks and temporal wasting.
Physical Exam: Integumentary exam reveals visible depletion of subcutaneous fat stores, particularly over the triceps and chest wall, with noticeable severe muscle wasting over the clavicles. Bilateral lower extremities demonstrate 3+ pitting peripheral oedema extending to the mid-calf. Cardiovascular exam reveals quiet, regular heart sounds with no S3 gallop and a flat jugular venous pressure, making acute heart failure unlikely. Respiratory exam is clear. Oral examination reveals poorly fitting upper dentures and severely inflamed gums. (Positive screening on the Mini Nutritional Assessment (MNA) and low serum albumin indicating severe protein-calorie deficit are anticipated diagnostic tetrad findings).
Environmental Observations: The refrigerator is mostly empty, containing only spoiled food; the patient is clearly unable to shop or cook.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications known to cause profound anorexia or severe oedema (e.g., calcium channel blockers are not present).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Protein-Calorie Malnutrition with secondary hypoalbuminaemic peripheral oedema - Status: Acute Decompensation
Differentials:

Acute Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of elevated JVP, crackles in the lungs, orthopnoea, or an S3 heart sound; the oedema is driven by low oncotic pressure (low albumin) rather than high hydrostatic pressure.

Chronic Kidney Disease (Nephrotic Syndrome) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: While possible, the profound, overriding clinical context of absolute starvation and cachexia makes primary severe malnutrition the most direct and pressing cause of the third-spacing.

Occult Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: While malignancy could drive the initial anorexia, the acute crisis is the physiological consequence of starvation (low albumin, oedema), and the clear mechanical barrier of severe dental pain offers a highly plausible primary etiology.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Inability to access or consume food safely)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with extreme lethargy and new-onset severe peripheral oedema in the context of profound, prolonged starvation due to dental pain and social isolation. Telephone triage correctly identified the risk of acute physiological collapse. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have left the patient without nutritional support, risking severe pressure necrosis due to immobility, cardiovascular collapse, and extreme distress. This urgent after-hours intervention allowed for the clinical differentiation between heart failure and hypoalbuminaemic oedema, the identification of a reversible mechanical cause for the starvation (dentures), and the immediate initiation of high-protein liquid supplementation and urgent social support to prevent a devastating hospital admission for starvation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed the patient to remove the ill-fitting dentures immediately to relieve oral pain. Commenced an immediate high-protein, high-calorie liquid nutritional supplement regimen (e.g., Ensure or Sustagen) to bypass the need for mastication, starting with very small, frequent volumes to avoid refeeding syndrome. Ordered urgent comprehensive bloods (FBC, CMP, Albumin, LFTs) for the morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a structured "sip and sit" routine. Step 1: The patient consumes 50mL of the high-protein liquid supplement every hour while sitting upright on the edge of the bed. Step 2: Upon successfully finishing the small hourly dose, the neighbor provides positive reinforcement by assisting the patient to elevate their legs on a pillow to reduce the swelling. Cause-and-effect: Linking the difficult task of consuming calories with the immediate, physically relieving reward of leg elevation ensures compliance with the strict, small-volume nutritional plan, slowly rebuilding albumin levels to draw the fluid out of the legs.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral generated for Meals on Wheels, an Aged Care Assessment Team (ACAT), and a community dietitian.
DME/Safety (Optional): Arranged for a pressure-relieving mattress overlay due to the extreme loss of subcutaneous fat.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 24-48 hours to assess urgent blood results (albumin/electrolytes) and monitor for refeeding syndrome.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department if the patient becomes completely unrousable, develops acute shortness of breath, experiences sudden severe abdominal pain, or exhibits muscle twitching/seizures (signs of severe electrolyte derangement).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe swelling in your legs is likely due to critically low protein levels in your blood from starvation, but urgent blood tests are required to confirm your kidneys and liver are functioning.
Clinical Course: The swelling will not resolve immediately. It will take several weeks of strict, high-protein liquid nutrition to rebuild the proteins in your blood that keep the fluid in your veins and out of your legs.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

(Directed to the neighbor): Call an ambulance immediately if the patient becomes impossible to wake up, starts breathing rapidly, or has a seizure. The GP must review the urgent blood tests within the next two days.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because your sore gums stopped you from eating, your body ran out of protein. Protein acts like a sponge in your blood, holding the water in. Without it, the water leaked out into your legs, causing the severe swelling. We are fixing this by giving you liquid protein that doesn't require chewing.
Actionable Steps: Leave your dentures out completely. Do not try to eat large meals. You must drink 50mL of the protein shake every single hour, and then elevate your legs. This slow, steady intake is the only safe way to rebuild your strength.

